now  suddenly things get old Jaggi  now what anti-aliasing does is try to  get rid of those jagged edges and make  everything look silky smooth by  changing the color of pixels around the  edge so that there's a gradual blend of  color interestingly you can turn this on  as an effect in Photoshop check it out  now if we zoom out a little much  smoother while anti-aliasing can have a  substantial effect on image quality it  is pretty heavy on the old processor  because has to render all those extra  color pixels thankfully though there are  a number of different types of  anti-aliasing to choose from multisample  anti-aliasing or MSA a this tries to be  smarter and less demanding on your  machine by applying itself only to the  parts of each frame that will look  jagged rather than the whole image it's  selective really on where and what it  works on fast approximate anti-aliasing  or fxaa  now this is the fastest and least  process heavy mode of all anti-aliasing  the reason is that it doesn't use high  resolution frame data instead it's  applied straight to the pixels on screen  at the resolution you're playing at it's  more blurry and doesn't look quite as  good but it's significantly less  demanding temporal anti-aliasing TX a a  sometimes mla a okay this is potentially  the best one this is a bit like ms AAA  but with an extra step you see TxAA  takes into account previous frames to  produce its own next frame by averaging  the colors also the final way to be  aliasing which i mentioned earlier is  our old friend resolution you just need  more of it check it out here we have  another lovely blue line except this  time it's at a much higher resolution in  fact this is at 4k this time if we turn  the high resolution line to the diagonal  without anti-aliasing well you don't  really notice those jaggies at all now  they are still there  if we zoom right in you can see but the  resolution is so great the size of each  pixel relative to the whole shape is  much smaller and so that stare effect is  really reduced the fact that 4k aliasing  pretty much stops being an issue  altogether  if you're trying to push your machine to  the limit with a brand new processor  intensive game aliasing is not the only  thing to worry about you've also got to  contend with the dreaded screen tear  screen tear happens when your graphics  card and your display aren't really on  the same page it's all to do with frame  rate and refresh rate so when you're  running around shooting wildlife in Far  Cry 3 your graphics card is hard at work  outputting frames at a hopefully steady  rate this is measured in frames per  second or fps your monitor on the other  hand is also refreshing its display at a  set rate per second called the refresh  rate now if these are out of sync it can  result in your display refreshing and  showing information from two or more  frames at the same time leading to the  appearance of nasty tears and this is  exactly what vsync aims to mitigate now  it does so by locking the frame rate  output of your graphics card to match  the refresh rate of your monitor in  reality though this does come with its  own issues as it can cause the gameplay  to stutter  whenever the GPU frame rate drops below  the display refresh rate this usually  happens when there's a whole lot of  stuff going on on-screen at once  interestingly though Nvidia have come up  with a cool new solution for this and is  called G sync the tag which is built  into special monitors enables the  display to refresh as soon as it gets a  new frame meaning it can match exactly  the output of the graphics card so with  G sync the monitor is keeping toe-to-toe  with a GPU rather than the other way  around this in theory lets you get the  absolute best performance and frame rate  from your machine  okay time to stop fixing problems and  start just making things look awesome  now all objects in video games are made  up of larger units called polygons  tessellation basically means taking a  polygon and breaking it up into smaller  bits so why is this useful well allows  your PC to generate greater detail in  the object using these smaller parts  it's particularly useful for generating  height and depth with tessellation  developers can apply displacement Maps  basically maps of height information to  a polygon and the tessellation will  calculate and generate the appropriate  level of depth making for more realistic  images this also helps with creating  nice smooth rounded objects  well I don't know about you guys but  that's about all the technical graphics  jargon I can handle for this episode so  make sure you come back next week when  we'll chat SSAO depth of field texture  filtering motion blur and maybe some  physics as well oh and for a deep dive  into frame rates and whether they matter  for gaming check at my previous episode  of reality check  do we need 60 FPS on ps4 and Xbox one  remember if you think I've missed  anything in this episode or if there's  anything in particular you like me to  mention next week do you let me know in  the comments down below or by dropping  me a tweet at cam 4 as Rob okay guys see  you next week
 

